## 2026-09-23 — Homepage hero: new footage, scrubbed instead of played
KEPT (user-requested replacement of the previous play-to-stops hero).
Footage: Zion Website/hero-section/Hero-new.mp4 — 1932x1080, 24fps, 8.4s.
Lobby push-in -> doors open -> through the car -> Zion COP panel.

Mechanism: the runway IS the timeline. gsap.ticker reads the section's
getBoundingClientRect each frame, lerps toward it (EASE 0.14) and writes
video.currentTime. Nothing autoplays; every frame shown is a frame the visitor
scrolled to, and scrolling up runs the lift back down.

Encoding: all-intra (-g 1 -keyint_min 1 -sc_threshold 0), 203/203 keyframes in
both encodes. Without this, seeking lands on the nearest keyframe and the scrub
stutters. Cost is size: CRF 26 @1920 = 8.9MB, CRF 27 @1280 = 4.4MB.

Lessons worth keeping:
- LEAD/TAIL (6%/8% of the runway) hold the first and last frames instead of
  flashing past them at the section edges.
- A scrubbed video is never played, so some browsers (iOS above all) decode
  nothing and paint blank. One muted play() immediately paused primes it.
- Progress is written as --p on the section, not React state: the grade, copy
  and progress line read it in CSS, so nothing re-renders per pixel of scroll.
- The old grade was built for dark footage. This take is lit warm marble, so the
  eyebrow and lead sat on bright stone until a left-hand scrim was added
  (bottom-only on phones, where the copy runs full width).
- Three stop-marks replaced by one continuous hairline: the film is continuous
  now, so the dots would have been describing a cut that no longer exists.

## 2026-09-23 — Homepage hero: editorial art direction over the scrubbed take
KEPT (user brief + reference image of a luxury-architecture hero).
Scroll-scrub mechanism from earlier today left completely intact; only the
composition, type and controls changed.

Composition: type moved from bottom-left to centre-left and set into the shadow
at the left of the lobby, where the grade already takes the frame down. The lift
stands centre-right and is never covered at any width.

Type: Fraunces 300 (already the site's editorial face, already loaded) at
clamp(2.3rem, 4.6vw, 4.5rem). Two sentences, the second in bronze. First pass
used 5.4vw in a 52vw column and the headline broke into four lines mid-sentence
— a masthead that wraps mid-sentence stops reading as one. Widened to 60vw and
eased to 4.6vw, which lands on the reference's three-line rhythm.

Two accents, one job each — worth keeping as a rule:
  bronze (#d2ae8a, drawn from the lobby's own door frame) = the brand's voice,
    never clickable. Headline accent, eyebrow rule, scroll stroke.
  teal = things you can act on. The live chapter mark, and focus rings.
This is why the headline is bronze and the live mark is teal, not the reverse.
Bronze is defined on `.hero` and nowhere else, so the site stays teal-only
below the fold — the user's long-standing no-gold rule still holds everywhere
except the one surface they asked for it on.

01–04: NOT slides. There is one continuous take, so four fake slide dots would
have been describing a carousel that does not exist. They are the points the
take turns on (approach / threshold / doors / cabin), they light as the scroll
reaches them, and they are real buttons that send the scroll back to that point.
Honest, and more useful than the decoration would have been.

Declined from the brief: the nav rewrite (HOME/RESOURCES/GET IN TOUCH). Nav is
site-wide and RESOURCES has no route — it would have been a link to nowhere on
every page. Flagged to the user rather than invented.

## 2026-09-23 — Hero: real image sequence, stripped frame, teal restored; new preloader
KEPT (user: "the hero section is acting like scroll scrub i want it to be
image sequence scroll only", plus removals, CTA redesign, accent change).

SEQUENCE (replaces the scrubbed <video> from earlier today)
135 WebP stills drawn to a <canvas>, one per scroll position. 1600-wide set
7.2MB, 900-wide 3.8MB — both lighter than the 8.9MB video they replace.
Why it is genuinely better, not just different: a still is either decoded or it
is not. Seeking a video can land a few tenths off the frame asked for and
arrives late on a flick; a still cannot. Proved it: scrolling to 0.25, away,
and back returns a byte-identical canvas. Video seeking could never guarantee
that.
Loading is coarse-to-fine — every 8th still, then 4th, 2nd, all — so the whole
length of the take is scrubbable within a second and merely sharpens as the
rest land, rather than the visitor waiting on an in-order load to reach the
part they already scrolled to. `nearest()` falls back to the closest arrived
still, so there is never a blank frame.

REMOVED from the frame, as asked: the VERTICAL TRANSPORTATION eyebrow, the
SCROLL cue, and the 01–04 marks. The frame is now the claim, the way in, and
the take. It is much stronger for it — worth remembering that the instinct to
furnish a hero with cues was wrong here.

ACCENT back to --teal-bright. The bronze experiment lasted one round; the
user's long-standing teal-only rule stands, and the headline now ties to the
"Lifts" in the nav. No bronze token remains anywhere.

CTA: the ring became a leader line — words, a rule running out from them, the
arrow at its end. On hover the rule draws further out and the arrow travels
with it, so the line itself is the movement and nothing has to glow.

PRELOADER: mark + wordmark hold on black, then the mark launches — straight up,
accelerating, plume behind it, ground falling away while it still climbs so the
launch reveals the page. The triangle was already a nose cone; the exit is the
one the shape asks for.
Two bugs worth remembering:
- Putting the arrival animation and the launch transform on ONE element makes
  the browser drop the transition and jump straight to the end, because the
  transform replaces a running animation inside the same style recalculation.
  Nest them: inner element arrives, outer element launches.
- The plume was drawn at the centre of the FRAME, but the lockup is a flex row,
  so the mark sits left of centre and the plume hung under the wordmark. It
  belongs to the mark, not the viewport — nest it in the mark's own box.

LEFT BEHIND: 20MB of now-unreferenced hero media (hero-1080/720.mp4,
hero-poster.jpg, hero-scrub-1080/720.mp4). Flagged to the user, not deleted.

## 2026-09-23 — Hero: glass pill CTA, scroll cue restored
KEPT (user supplied the reference button markup/CSS and an image of the cue).

CTA: the leader line became the reference's glass pill — label, then the action
held in a filled disc at the end. Kept the reference's material logic (lit rim
from inset highlights on two corners, blurred diagonal sheen on ::after,
backdrop-blur) but retuned the values for a DARK ground; at the reference's own
numbers, which assume a light page, it read as a white slab on the film. The
disc takes the teal, consistent with teal = the thing you can do.

Dropped from the reference on purpose: `filter: contrast(3)` on a wrapper. It
is what gives that demo its liquid-glass rim, but it crushes the label's colour
and interacts badly with backdrop-filter over a canvas. Said so rather than
shipping it degraded.

CUE: the mouse-and-wheel indicator is back at the foot of the frame, centred,
fading out by 10% of the runway. Removed it two rounds ago at the user's
request and they asked for it back — noted, the cue earns its place.

BUILD BUG WORTH REMEMBERING
`backdrop-filter` silently vanished from the built CSS. Cause: I wrote the
standard property FIRST and `-webkit-` second; the minifier treats the two as
aliases and keeps only the last, so the standard property was dropped and only
the webkit form shipped. The rest of this codebase writes `-webkit-` first,
standard second. Follow that order everywhere.
Also: `getComputedStyle(el).backdropFilter` returns the STRING "none" when
unset, which is truthy — `a || b` fallbacks in a check will never reach `b`.
That nearly hid the bug.

TEST NOTE: the reverse-determinism check on the sequence is settle-sensitive.
The lerp (EASE 0.14) needs ~2.5s to converge; at 1.4s it can sit one frame off
and the canvas fingerprint differs. Not a bug — give it the time before
calling a mismatch real.

## 2026-09-23 — /projects case studies: the held stage (KEPT — pending user)
Rejections before this, in order: traction wheel, lift ride, 3D drag wall,
photographic city, horizontal drag strips, alternating image/copy register
("AI slop"), drawing sheets ("change the design").

THE FEEDBACK THAT FINALLY MATTERED: "look how the homepage and other pages are
and then change the design". Every rejected attempt invented a NEW section
language. The site already had one and I was not using it.

The site's signature (`.world` / WorldBelow in home.css + Ascent.jsx):
  sticky pin + runway, full-bleed photographic layers crossfading with a small
  scale, a left veil so type sits across the photo's edge, a copy block that
  re-enters on each change via gsap.fromTo stagger, a specification strip along
  the foot, and a numbered rail you can click.

The case studies are now built in exactly that language (`.pw`), so the page
reads as part of the site instead of a portfolio grid bolted onto it.
One deliberate difference so it is family and not a clone: on the home page the
title is FIXED and contexts change under it; here the headline IS the building,
set in Fraunces and changing with the photograph, because the building is the
subject rather than an example of one. The rail is grouped by sector.

This also finally kills the original defect: healthcare has ONE case study, so
every strip/grid attempt wrapped a scroller around a single card. On a rail,
one case study is one line. The content problem dissolved once the container
was right.

SELF-CRITIQUE of the register that was called slop, recorded so it is not
repeated: Specificity 1 (pasteable into any agency site unchanged), Philosophy 1
(no position, just a competent layout), Variety 2 (alternating rows is THE
default shape). It also failed tastemaker gate 6 outright — zero intentional
rule-breaks, everything on grid, evenly spaced, perfectly safe.

LESSON, the important one: on this project, "unique" does not mean a new
mechanism. Five bespoke mechanisms were rejected. What works is the site's own
established language carrying real content. Extend, do not invent.

Also this round: the preloader progress bar was removed at the user's request;
the hold is now carried by a teal charge glow gathering under the mark and
spending itself at the launch, which suits the rocket rather than competing
with it.

## 2026-09-23 — Homepage lifts section (.lx): the rail slimmed, the type seated
KEPT (user supplied a detailed spec + a gold reference mockup).

The reference mockup was GOLD; the live section was already teal. Kept teal —
the user asked for the site's blue, and no gold token exists in this codebase.

RAIL: was a 4.25rem column carrying a name under all nine marks, which read as
a sidebar and crowded the room behind it. Now 64px wide, 51px clear of the edge,
marks only, generous spacing, 1.6rem radius, 20px backdrop blur, active mark in
teal with a soft ring. The name comes out on a flag to the LEFT on hover/focus,
so it stays discoverable without being permanent furniture.

REMOVED the hairline that ran beside the rail (and its GSAP tween + ref). The
04/09 at the foot already says where you are; saying it twice was the clutter.

GRADE: the left scrim went from 0.78→0.5@26% to 0.9→0.74@24%→0.34@44%. The
copy had been sitting on two figures in the photograph. The lift itself, centre
-right, is still untouched.

TWO REGRESSIONS I INTRODUCED AND BACKED OUT — worth remembering:
Chasing the brief's "approximately 2 lines", I clamped the description to 3
lines and the drive value to 2. Both then truncated mid-word with an ellipsis
("...retrofits and heavy…", "with submersib…"). Losing real copy is a worse
trade than a slightly taller block. The compactness has to come from the
measure and the size, never from cutting the sentence off.

PARALLAX: the brief asked for subtle image parallax. It already exists — the
timeline scales each room 1.06 → 1.0 across its own segment, scrubbed by
scroll. Did not add a second one.

ICONS: the brief asked to use public/media/lift-icons/*.svg. The rail already
renders that same artwork via components/lift-marks.jsx, normalised to a shared
1000-unit square and drawn in currentColor. The raw files are fill="#000" with
mismatched viewBoxes (e.g. "287 32 1624 1624"), so using them directly would
render black — unable to take the teal active state — at nine different optical
sizes, plus nine extra requests. Kept lift-marks; told the user why.

## 2026-09-23 — Installations map: choosing a place now actually zooms into it
Clicking a PIN already zoomed. Choosing an AREA from the list often did not:
Kompally, Jubilee Hills and others moved the map slightly and looked inert.

Cause: `flyToBounds` fits whatever bounds it is handed, and an area whose
installations are spread across the city fits at roughly the zoom the city view
already sits at. Leaflet's bounds options carry `maxZoom` but there is no
`minZoom` — the fit can only be capped from above, never floored.

Fix: work out the fitting zoom with `map.getBoundsZoom(bounds, false)` first,
then clamp it from BOTH ends — never further out than 13.5, never closer than
16 — and `flyTo(center, z)`. Single installations go to a firm 16, street level.
Bounds padding dropped from 0.3 to 0.12 since the clamp now does that work.

TESTING NOTE worth keeping: reading the map's zoom from the FIRST `.leaflet-tile`
in the DOM is unreliable — stale tiles from the previous level are retained
during a fly, so a working zoom reads as "no change". Take the mode of all
`.leaflet-tile-loaded` URLs instead. A second false failure came from holding
Playwright element handles across a React re-render (`showAll` clears the
search and rebuilds the list); re-query by name each time.
